  "summary": "A direct, physical approach to painting where the artist's bodily movements are part of the creative process.",
  "description": "Characterized by energetic, sweeping brushstrokes and dripped paint applied with forceful gestures, reflecting the artist's physical engagement with the canvas. Developed as part of the Abstract Expressionist movement, it emphasized spontaneity and the subconscious over pre-planned compositions.",
  "culturalContext": "Action Painting — Harold Rosenberg's 1952 coinage for Pollock, de Kooning, Kline and the New York School's gesture wing — treated canvas as \"an arena in which to act\": dripped skeins, slashed strokes, full-body scale, and the record of performance as the artwork. The style shifted art's center from Paris to New York, was filmed (Namuth's Pollock footage) as event, and launched Happenings, performance art and every \"the process is the product\" pedagogy.",
